//? Title: Fermat Primality: Witnesses, Base Populations and Actual Rounds
//? Subtitle: Exact modular powers, native liar censuses and the limits of a seeded probable-prime result
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Decide whether an unsigned integer can be ruled out as prime without first
finding all of its factors. Fermat's little theorem supplies a cheap
compositeness certificate: one failed modular congruence. Passing bases
provide no converse certificate. We begin with four exact base tests for
15, retain the original prime/composite/Carmichael examples and their five
coprime-liar fractions, then inspect actual seeded rounds. Native APIs
replace the report-local powering and census loops. The report distinguishes
conditional coprime fractions, the sampler's full support and hypothetical
independent draws, and checks full-width arithmetic and capture limits.
)

\toc

> A failure proves compositeness; a pass does not prove primality

For a prime p and 1≤a<p, multiplication by a permutes the nonzero residues.
Multiplying all residues before and after the permutation and cancelling
(p−1)! gives a^(p−1)=1 modulo p. Thus a^(n−1)≠1 modulo n proves n
composite. A pass is only consistency with that necessary condition.
For 15, base two gives 2^14 modulo 15 equal to four: a witness with gcd one.
Base ten also fails, and its gcd five directly gives a factor. Bases four
and eleven pass despite 15=3 times five being composite.

The native single-base API computes the exact modular power through mod_pow
and the actual gcd. The diagram and table use its returned records. The
explicit-base range is [1,n); the seeded sampler later uses [2,n−2].

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fermat_primality_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
