> Preserve the original five liar fractions, with explicit denominators

A “liar” is a passing base for a known composite. For a prime, passing bases
are expected, so the table uses the neutral label “passes”. The original
report considered 2≤a<n and counted only coprime bases in its denominator.
That interval excludes one but includes n−1. The new native census makes
both interval endpoints explicit and returns integer counts first.

For 45 the original fraction is 7/23; for 91 it is 35/71. The Carmichael
numbers 561 and 1105 have conditional fraction one, as does prime 97.
The plot deliberately conditions on coprimality and labels that choice.
It does not depict the probability that the native sampler passes a round.
No census or modular exponentiation is reimplemented in the report.

