> Carmichael numbers fool coprime tests, not every sampled base

A passing residue must be coprime: if a prime divisor of n divides a,
it divides a^(n−1), so that residue cannot be one. Carmichael numbers pass
for every coprime base, while their noncoprime bases still fail. For 561,
the factors 3,11,17 are distinct primes and each p−1 divides 560. Applying
the prime congruence in each factor and combining residues explains why
every coprime base passes modulo 561. The report checks these factors and
divisibilities, then computes the actual two populations.

The native sampler draws from [2,n−2], represented as the half-open
interval [2,n−1). For odd n this removes the always-passing endpoints one
and n−1. For 561, there are 318 passes among 558 possible sampled bases,
whereas all 318 coprime bases in that support pass. Those are different
fractions. Native exact censuses below make the distinction visible.

The seeded sequence uses SplitMix64 words reduced modulo n−3. A fixed
seed is deterministic; modulo reduction generally introduces bias, and
this generator does not establish independent uniform draws. Therefore
neither the following population fractions nor powers of them are a
rigorous error bound for the actual seeded API. They describe specified
populations and, later, an explicitly hypothetical iid sampling model.

