\caption(The native census supplies the population fractions; the curves apply an explicitly hypothetical independent uniform sampling model. Executed quota,ownership,domain and same-base stronger-test controls accompany the model.)
\alttext(A native plot for five-hundred-sixty-one keeps the coprime-conditioned passing probability at one while a hypothetical uniform-support probability decays as its measured census fraction is raised to the number of independent rounds.)

> API contracts and verification

The bool entry retains its compiled signature, defaults and original positive
round stream. It now rejects nonpositive round counts before filtering;
the old vacuous odd-input true for zero rounds was not evidence. The work
and trace variants expose actual filters, round counts and residues. The
single-base API requires n≥2 and 1≤base<n. The census requires a valid
half-open interval 1≤first≤last≤n; [n,n) is a valid empty interval. It
returns integer counts and leaves empty-denominator fractions undefined.

Development checks preserve 19200 frozen positive-round decisions, including
all original inputs, full-width values and seeded cases, and all 13 lines
of original program stdout. Strict sanitizer tests compare small powers
against repeated multiplication, split exact censuses into disjoint ranges,
check golden seeded prefixes, actual early stops, capture/work caps and
ownership. An optional independent Python pow/gcd oracle checks 42710
base results; it is not a report runtime dependency. The public Doxygen
examples are compiled and their output checked separately.

Keith Conrad, [Fermat's Test](https://kconrad.math.uconn.edu/blurbs/ugradnumthy/fermattest.pdf),
sections1–3, explains congruence witnesses and why shared divisors can expose
compositeness. The native single-base records show both tests explicitly.

Victor Shoup, [A Computational Introduction to Number Theory and Algebra,
version2](https://shoup.net/ntb/ntb-v2.pdf), 2008, section10.2, Theorem10.1
and the Carmichael discussion on pp.307–308, supplies the subgroup setting
for the conditional counting argument. Native modulo-reduced seeded draws
are distinguished from the book's ideal random-sampling model.
